{{also|pus}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|po͝os}}, {{IPA|/pʊs/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ʊs|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From a Common {{etyl|gem|en}} word for cat, perhaps ultimately imitative of a sound made to get its attention. Akin to {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|poes||puss, cat, vagina}}, West Frisian {{m|fy|poes}}, {{etyl|nds|-}} {{m|nds|Puus}}, {{m|nds|Puuskatte}}, Danish {{m|da|pus}}, dialectal {{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|kattepus}}, {{etyl|no|-}} {{m|no|pus}}.

Found also in several other European, North Africa and West Asian languages. Compare {{etyl|ro|-}} {{m|ro|pisica}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{context|informal|lang=en}} A [[cat]].
#: ''Our local theatre is showing Puss in Boots.''
# A girl or young woman.
# {{context|dated|hunting|lang=en}} A [[hare]].<!--Webster 1913-->
# {{context|vulgar|slang|lang=en}} [[vulva|Vulva]] (female genitalia).

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|cat}} [[moggie]]/[[moggy]]

=====Related terms=====
* [[pussy]]

===Etymology 2===
Of {{etyl|cel|en}} origin, from or akin to {{etyl|ga|en}} {{term|pus||lang=ga|mouth, lip}}, from {{etyl|mga|en}} {{term|bus||lang=mga}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{context|slang|lang=en}} The [[mouth]].
#: ''She gave him a slap in the '''puss'''.''

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|mouth}} [[cakehole]], [[gob]], [[mush]], [[trap]]

===Anagrams===
* [[PSUs#English|PSUs]], [[sups#English|sups]], [[USPs#English|USPs]], [[USPS#English|USPS]]

